**Context (TS 38.331, clause on SLRB configuration for NR sidelink communication):**

For each sidelink DRB (SL‑DRB) that needs to be established or modified as a result of receiving `sl‑ConfigDedicatedNR`, `SIB12`, or `SidelinkPreconfigNR`, the UE creates/updates an entry in `slrb-ConfigToAddModList` and, in that entry, sets:

- **`sl-DRB-Identity`** – to identify the sidelink DRB;
- **`sl-SDAP-Config`** – the SDAP configuration for the SL‑DRB;
- **`sl-PDCP-Config`** – the PDCP configuration for the SL‑DRB;
- **`sl-RLC-Config`** (per associated RLC channel/leg) – the RLC bearer configuration;
- **`sl-MAC-LogicalChannelConfig`** – the logical channel configuration associated with the SL‑DRB.

These fields are populated using the values received in the corresponding configuration (`sl-ConfigDedicatedNR`, `SIB12`, or `SidelinkPreconfigNR`); if a given field is not explicitly signalled, the UE applies the corresponding default configuration as specified in TS 38.331 (Clause 9, default SL‑RLC/PDCP/logical‑channel configurations) for that SL‑DRB.

**Purpose (as stated in 3GPP TS 38.331):**

"The purpose of this procedure is:

- to inform NAS about paging;
- to trigger RRC connection establishment for RRC_IDLE or RRC_INACTIVE UEs;
- to inform RRC_IDLE and RRC_INACTIVE UEs about system information change;
- to inform RRC_IDLE and RRC_INACTIVE UEs about an ETWS indication;
- to inform RRC_IDLE and RRC_INACTIVE UEs about a CMAS indication;
- to inform RRC_IDLE and RRC_INACTIVE UEs about EAB parameters change;
- to inform applicable UEs in RRC_IDLE, RRC_INACTIVE and RRC_CONNECTED state about a public warning message related to an outage of the RAN notification area subject to public warning system service continuity."

This is used by the network to page the UE, both for mobile-terminating calls/data and for delivering system information or public-warning-related notifications.

The purpose of the measurement configuration is described in TS 38.331 as follows:

**"The network provides the measurement configuration applicable for a UE by means of dedicated signalling, i.e. using the RRCReconfiguration or, for conditional reconfiguration, the RRCReconfiguration or RRCResume message. With the measConfig the network configures the UE with:
1> measurement objects: The objects on which the UE shall perform the measurements;
2> reporting configurations: A list of reporting configurations where each reporting configuration includes the following information:
- reporting criteria: The criteria that trigger the UE to send a measurement report. This can either be periodical or a single event description;
- RS type: The RS that the UE uses for beam-specific measurements, and cell measurement results based on SS/PBCH block or CSI-RS;
- reporting format: The quantities per cell and per beam that the UE includes in the measurement report and associated information (e.g. the number of cells and beams to report);
3> measurement identities: A list of measurement identities where each measurement identity links one measurement object with one reporting configuration.**"

**Source: TS 38.331, clause 5.4.3.5 “Reception of the MobilityFromNRCommand message by the UE” (failure case)**

According to the specification, when the UE fails to establish the connection to the target RAT after receiving a *MobilityFromNRCommand* with *targetRAT‑Type* set to *eutra*, and the UE supports the RLF Report for Inter‑RAT MRO (EUTRA) feature, the UE performs the following actions:

> "1> if the UE does not succeed in establishing the connection to the target radio access technology:
> 2> if the *targetRAT‑Type* is set to *eutra*, and if the UE supports RLF Report for inter‑RAT MRO EUTRA:
> 3> set the *connectionFailureType* to *hof* in the *VarRLF‑Report*;
> 3> set the *failedPCellId* to the global cell identity and tracking area code, if available, or to the physical cell identity and the carrier frequency of the target PCell, of the E‑UTRA cell that the UE tried to connect to;
> 3> set the *previousPCellId* to the global cell identity, if available, or to the physical cell identity and the carrier frequency, of the source NR PCell;
> 3> set the *measResultLastServCell* to include the RSRP and RSRQ, if available, of the source NR PCell based on the last measurement results taken before the handover;
> 3> set the *measResultNeighCells*, if available, to include the best measured cells, other than the source NR PCell and target E-UTRA cell, ordered such that the best cell is listed first, in *VarRLF‑Report*, in accordance with 5.3.10.5;
> 3> set the *timeConnFailure* to the time elapsed since the last MobilityFromNRCommand until connection failure occurred, if available;
> 3> set the *locationInfo*, if available, in accordance with 5.3.3.7;
> 2> revert back to the configuration used in the source PCell, excluding SCell(s) and, if configured, the configuration used in the CG-SCG;
> 2> initiate the connection re-establishment procedure as specified in clause 5.3.7, upon which the fields set in *VarRLF‑Report* are used for reporting the connection (handover) failure to the network."

In short: the UE records the failure details (setting *connectionFailureType* = *hof*, and populating *failedPCellId*, *previousPCellId*, *measResultLastServCell*, *measResultNeighCells*, *timeConnFailure*, and *locationInfo* in the *VarRLF-Report*), reverts to the source PCell configuration, and then triggers RRC connection re-establishment, using the stored VarRLF-Report information for subsequent RLF/MRO reporting.

**Reference:** 3GPP TS 38.331, clause 5.3.13.5 "Cell re-selection while T319 is running"

**Exact statement (as specified in the standard):**

> "Upon cell re-selection while T319 is running, the UE shall:
> 1> perform the actions as specified in 5.3.13.6, as if T319 expires."

In other words, the UE does **not** wait for T319 to actually time out; instead, cell reselection while T319 is running triggers the UE to immediately execute the same actions defined for **T319 expiry** (clause 5.3.13.6). These actions include, among others:

- Stopping timer T319 (if not already expired);
- Discarding the stored *resumeIdentity*, the current K_gNB, the AS security context, and any current AS security context resulted from the resume attempt;
- Reverting to using the RRC configuration used prior to initiation of the RRC Resume procedure (except for NCC, if applicable);
- Indicating to the upper layers that the suspended RRC connection resume attempt has failed;
- Performing the actions specified in clause 5.3.13 (i.e., moving to RRC_IDLE) with release cause "RRC connection failure" (as applicable).

Essentially, cell reselection during T319 is treated by the UE identically to a T319 expiry event.

The network applies the RRC connection establishment procedure, instead of resume/re-establishment, in the following case:

**"The network applies the procedure as follows:**
- **the same or different gNB than the one which the UE had the RRC connection previously may respond to the RRC re-establishment request, as well as to the resume request;
- if the network accepts the RRC re-establishment or resume request, the network applies the procedures as specified in clause 5.3.7.3 (RRC re-establishment) or 5.3.13 (RRC resume), respectively;
- if the network rejects the RRC re-establishment or resume request, the network typically applies the procedures as specified in clause 5.3.7.4 (RRC connection re-establishment rejection) or 5.3.13.5 (resume rejection);
- if it is not possible for the network to determine the UE context based on the ResumeIdentity or on the target cell C-RNTI and PCI included in the RRCReestablishmentRequest / RRCResumeRequest / RRCResumeRequest1, the network applies the procedure as if the UE was in RRC_IDLE and initiates the RRC connection establishment procedure"**

In other words: **when the network cannot identify/retrieve the UE context based on the information (ResumeIdentity, or PCI and C-RNTI of the source cell) provided in the RRCResumeRequest, RRCResumeRequest1, or RRCReestablishmentRequest message**, it falls back to treating the UE as if it were in RRC_IDLE, and applies the RRC connection establishment procedure instead.
